 STT's taint propagation skips any source register with index 16 (\texttt{rob\_impl.hh:229})\footnote{File:line references are to each defense's gem5 source: STT's repository at commit e3283b9~\cite{sttrepo}, ReCon's implementation as obtained from its authors, and AMuLeT's artifact build of SpecLFB~\cite{amulet}.}, a check meant for the zero register that ignores the register class. Floating-point index 16 is the low half of \texttt{xmm4}, so a speculatively loaded secret copied through \texttt{xmm4} and back loses its taint, and the dependent transmitter accesses the cache inside the violation window. Of 100 load-to-load tests that STT blocks when every value stays in an integer register, 98 leak when the value of the load that reads the secret is moved through \texttt{xmm4} before its use, and none when it is moved through \texttt{xmm3}. The check is also at line 229 of STT's master branch, commit 5bf4110~\cite{sttmaster}.
