-\subsubsection{UV3: ReCon: Only The Oldest Taint Source}\label{sec:bug-otb}
+% [F1, cut plan 2026-11-16: was \subsubsection{UV3: ReCon: Only The Oldest Taint Source}; bold run-in head instead]
+\noindent\textbf{UV3: ReCon: Only The Oldest Taint Source.}\label{sec:bug-otb}
 % [lead for Sec.~\ref{sec:bug-otb}] (2026-11-10: the detail subsubsection is commented out below; its label moved here)
 STT keeps a value tainted until the youngest load it depends on reaches the visibility point~\cite[\S 7.1]{yu2019stt}. ReCon's implementation of STT~\cite{recon} instead keeps one taint source per register: an instruction with several tainted operands passes on only the oldest one's taint (\texttt{taint\_tracker.cc:108--131}), which is dropped once that source stops being speculative. A transmitter fed by an older and a younger speculative load is thus released while the younger one is still speculative: Fig.~\ref{fig:otb} shows this in generated test inst-003908. The release needs the older load's branch to resolve after the merge is renamed, since ReCon assigns taint at rename: without the swept 500-cycle hold, i2 would resolve at cycle~6, before the merge is renamed at cycle~121, and ReCon would hold i7 (Table~\ref{tab:instrtable}, row~2).
