 17 of the 30{,}368 tests from our STT model show this bug on ReCon's implementation of STT. In all 17, the older operand reaches the transmitter only through register reads that gem5's x86 instructions make and the x86 specification does not~\cite{intelsdm}: a \texttt{mov} or \texttt{cqto} reading its old destination (12 tests, as in Fig.~\ref{fig:otb}), or an arithmetic instruction reading the old flags (5). ReCon records one taint source per register (\texttt{taint\_tracker.hh:27}) and gives an instruction's result only its oldest tainted source (\texttt{taint\_tracker.cc:111}), so an instruction that reads two tainted registers, such as an \texttt{add}, loses the younger source in the same way.
