+\noindent\textbf{UV4: SpecLFB: ROB Walk Order.}\label{sec:uv4-speclfb-rob-walk} This bug widens A-UV6: AMuLeT reports SpecLFB's unprotected load only for the first speculative load~\cite[\S 4.7]{amulet}, and its patch for A-UV6 would also close this bug. SpecLFB~\cite{cheng2024speclfb} decides whether a speculative load is unsafe in two walks: the ROB walk marks a load that has no older unsafe load, and the LSQ walk flags loads in a branch's shadow. A load is held only if it is flagged and was not marked. At gem5's default delays, a load reaches the ROB one cycle before it reaches the load queue, so the ROB walk can mark a younger load before the LSQ walk has flagged the older one, and the mark is never cleared. Loads after the first speculative load can therefore go unprotected as well, whereas AMuLeT reports the leak only for the first one (A-UV6). In 398 of the first 4{,}000 tests, only a later load leaks.
+
+% [F1, cut plan 2026-11-16: was \subsubsection{UV5: SpecLFB: The Shadow Of A \texttt{jmp}}; bold run-in head instead]
+\noindent\textbf{UV5: SpecLFB: The Shadow Of A \texttt{jmp}.}\label{sec:uv5-speclfb-shadow-jmp}
 % [cut 2026-11-16, Anna: narrative does not need it] The logic was ported from DOLMA~\cite{loughlin2021dolma}, which does not have this bug. 
 SpecLFB treats a load as safe only if no older control instruction is unresolved~\cite[\S 6.2.2]{cheng2024speclfb}, and its ROB walk tracks this with a single flag. An unresolved branch clears the flag, but a younger direct \texttt{jmp} or \texttt{call} sets it again (\texttt{rob.cc:501}), erasing the older branch's shadow, so loads after the jump count as safe and fill the L1D speculatively. A test reaches it when a direct jump lies between the mispredicted branch and a later load: always with a flushed or filled cache. When we run AMuLeT's fuzzer on SpecLFB, it also triggers this bug, in 47 of 248 violations, which AMuLeT's analysis would attribute to A-UV6 (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
 
-\subsubsection{UV6: The Visibility-Point/Squash Race}\label{sec:bug-race}
+% [F1, cut plan 2026-11-16: was \subsubsection{UV6: The Visibility-Point/Squash Race}; bold run-in head instead]
+\noindent\textbf{UV6: The Visibility-Point/Squash Race.}\label{sec:bug-race}
 % [Sec.~\ref{sec:bug-race}, moved last]. (2026-11-10: the detail subsubsection is commented out below; its label moved here)
 % [before 2026-11-16: depends on where the window ends] and in that gap the transmitter accesses the cache inside the violation window, which lasts until the squash lands.
 STT~\cite{yu2019stt} releases a transmitter once the branch it depends on resolves, even on the wrong path: a wrong-path load's result stops being tainted as soon as every older branch has resolved (\texttt{rob\_impl.hh:812}). The squash triggered by that resolution reaches the issue stage only \texttt{commitToIEWDelay} cycles later, and in that gap the transmitter obtains its data. We claim the race only for squashes slower than gem5's default (\S\ref{sec:discussion}): \texttt{commitToIEWDelay}~$=3$ triggers it on STT, and at the default of~1 the squash arrives first. SPT-Fence~\cite{spt} lifts its fence on the same signal and races the same way (on tests of SPT's own specification at \texttt{commitToIEWDelay}~$=4$, in 1{,}674 of 2{,}000, and in none of 2{,}000 at the default; re-running 40 of them with the campaign's timing confirms the full signature in all 40, as in Fig.~\ref{fig:race}).
